[
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"110087\">@Kim Morrison</span> tagging because you wrote the original PRs. Claude code surfaced a potential issue with linarith? and also provided a fix. I am still in the process of building my expertise in Lean but want to run it by you if the issue makes sense. <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/issues/41471\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/issues/41471</a></p>",
        "id": 608839153,
        "sender_full_name": "Fawad",
        "timestamp": 1783459456
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>this should not fail or work like linarith. </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Tactic</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Linarith</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℚ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hu</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hv</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">linarith?</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- fails</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℚ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">gu</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">linarith?</span><span class=\"w\">     </span><span class=\"c1\">-- fails</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 608839778,
        "sender_full_name": "Fawad",
        "timestamp": 1783459721
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The second one looks like a bug to me: adding even a single unused variable makes <code>linarith?</code> fail.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Tactic</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Linarith</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/--</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">info: Try this:</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">  [apply] linarith only [h]</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">-/</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard_msgs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℚ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">linarith?</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">/--</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">error: linarith failed to find a contradiction</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">a u : ℚ</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">h : a &lt; 0</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">a✝ : 0 &lt; a</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">⊢ False</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">failed</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">-/</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard_msgs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℚ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">linarith?</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- Comparison, linarith works fine on this example.</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℚ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hu</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">linarith</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 608840600,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1783460055
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I will issue a PR written by Claude. Folks more capable than me in this area can review it</p>",
        "id": 608840863,
        "sender_full_name": "Fawad",
        "timestamp": 1783460180
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>That said: reporting an issue that AI told you about without understanding it yourself is... not well received in many open source projects these days. The same goes for PRs. The reason is reviewer capacity: it's easy to make such PRs, but takes much longer to review them (and reviewer capacity is already at an absolute premium in mathlib). <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/contribute/#use-of-ai\">#use-of-ai</a> spells this out in more detail.</p>",
        "id": 608841137,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1783460298
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks for pointing that out actually. I will take a couple of days to really review and absorb it. I understand the issue of course but have less understanding of the fix. After that I will issue a PR</p>",
        "id": 608841669,
        "sender_full_name": "Fawad",
        "timestamp": 1783460474
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Why do you think that your AI will make a better PR than anyone else's (including, for example, the people who wrote <code>linarith</code>'s)? Why not just stick with the issue rather than potentially causing more work?</p>",
        "id": 608842589,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1783460831
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In fact if you want to be more helpful (finding the issue is already helpful but a slop PR is not really) then you could rewrite the issue to be about 10% of its current size (it's currently one of those super-lengthy AI-generated extremely waffly efforts) and to get to the point far more quickly by just showing short failing examples.</p>",
        "id": 608848119,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1783463877
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"110038\">@Kevin Buzzard</span> I updated the issue. The bug in question was introduced by code written by Codex. I never said that my PR can be better than someone else's. The aggressive tone of your remark is unnecessary. I will not issue a PR, that is completely fine.</p>",
        "id": 608987677,
        "sender_full_name": "Fawad",
        "timestamp": 1783517769
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Sorry, I didn't mean to be aggressive. I am just honestly completely bewildered about how people (and you are certainly not the first) can come here and say \"hi, I have no understanding of Lean but I am going to make a PR to your professionally-written repo anyway\". Do they honestly think that a non-expert armed with an LLM can do something strictly better than an expert armed with an LLM? Can they not see that actually they are wasting people's time?</p>\n<p>I am not talking about the issue -- it's great that you reported it. But why do you think it's your job to fix it? I just don't get how people are coming to this conclusion.</p>",
        "id": 609000470,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1783520690
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I imagine it may be because they believe that the expert has better and more important things to do that they (ask a LLM to) write a fix</p>",
        "id": 609022575,
        "sender_full_name": "Edward van de Meent",
        "timestamp": 1783526047
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>And they think that's acceptable since it is an open project</p>",
        "id": 609022715,
        "sender_full_name": "Edward van de Meent",
        "timestamp": 1783526086
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Valid criticism. This needs a more structured, engineering management solution than this exchange I think. Just to clarify I have been working in Lean for the past 6 months continuously. Anyhoo I don't want to prolong this discussion, some PRs need to be reviewed :) Was reading the book by Kevin Hartnett. Thanks for bringing Lean 4 to the to the world.</p>",
        "id": 609069471,
        "sender_full_name": "Fawad",
        "timestamp": 1783538140
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think I found where the bug is for this - will open a PR with the fix!</p>",
        "id": 609194973,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Lezeau",
        "timestamp": 1783593806
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Has there been any progress on this? I would be interested in having a go at patching this (and getting experience with how Lean tactics work underneath). My understanding for what's wrong is there's a bug in <code>Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith.Frontend</code> as follows:</p>\n<p>In <code>linarithUsedHyps</code>, after a goal is achieved, the list of facts used is assembled like so:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hyps</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">←</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">only_on</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">then</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">return</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">new_var.toList</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">++</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hyps</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"k\">else</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">return</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">←</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">getLocalHyps</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">toList</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">++</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hyps</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The non-<code>only_on</code> path returns every local hypothesis as an <code>Expr</code>, including plain variable declarations. Hence, <code>(x y u v : ℚ)</code>, <code>(hu : u &lt; v)</code>, <code>(hv : v &lt; u)</code>and <code>y ≤ x</code>are all introduced (7 altogether). </p>\n<p>This list is given to <code>runLinarith</code>, which runs this line</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">branches</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">←</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">preprocess</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">preprocessors</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hyps</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>preprocess</code> runs a preprocessor, which has the <code>defaultPreprocessor</code> as default, which contains these:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">defaultPreprocessors</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">GlobalBranchingPreprocessor</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">filterComparisons</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">nnrealToReal</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">natToInt</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">strengthenStrictInt</span><span class=\"o\">,</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">compWithZero</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cancelDenoms</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The <code>filterComparisons</code> is the one which will cause issues since this can filter out the non-comparison Exprs, which is a very reasonable thing to do. This leaves the list of exprs as <code>(hu : u &lt; v)</code>, <code>(hv : v &lt; u)</code>and <code>y ≤ x</code> (3 altogether). But we then attach indices to this filtered list:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">for</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">es</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">branches</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">do</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">esIdx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">es.zipIdx</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">idxs</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">←</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">singleProcess</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">esIdx</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>These indices get threaded through the rest of the pipeline here and the relevant indices are passed back to <code>linarithUsedHyps</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">usedIdxs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">←</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">runLinarith</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cfg</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">target_type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hyps</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">used</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">usedIdxs.filterMap</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hyps</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">?</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>But <code>hyps</code> is the original list, not the preprocessed list, and so the used hypotheses it thinks are just these type <code>ℚ</code> exprs.</p>\n<p>I haven't tried fixing it yet, but it seems relatively straightforward.</p>",
        "id": 612140465,
        "sender_full_name": "Sharvil Kesarwani",
        "timestamp": 1784728460
    }
]